1
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,345
It is three in the morning, logins are down, and forty minutes later you are left

2
00:00:10,345 --> 00:00:13,350
with five different log files and a postmortem to write.

3
00:00:13,350 --> 00:00:17,320
Postmortem Pilot turns those raw logs into an evidence grounded, blameless

4
00:00:17,320 --> 00:00:18,875
postmortem in about a minute.

5
00:00:20,875 --> 00:00:25,972
This sample incident has a Postgres log, backup tool output, Kubernetes events, the

6
00:00:25,972 --> 00:00:28,182
auth service log, and alert history.

7
00:00:28,182 --> 00:00:33,278
It also contains a database password and a bearer token, and a frontend deploy that

8
00:00:33,278 --> 00:00:36,839
happened at the same time, which is a classic red herring.

9
00:00:38,839 --> 00:00:42,502
First, secrets are redacted locally, before any model call.

10
00:00:42,502 --> 00:00:47,717
Then NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Nano, running on Nebius Token Factory, reads every log source

11
00:00:47,717 --> 00:00:52,808
in parallel and extracts only the events that matter, each with a reference to its

12
00:00:52,808 --> 00:00:53,491
exact line.

13
00:00:55,491 --> 00:00:58,034
The expensive model never reads the raw dump.

14
00:00:58,034 --> 00:01:02,555
Nemotron 3 Ultra, the five hundred fifty billion parameter reasoning model, gets

15
00:01:02,555 --> 00:01:05,437
only the merged timeline and the lines that matter.

16
00:01:05,437 --> 00:01:10,128
It separates trigger from root cause, builds the causal chain, and explicitly rules

17
00:01:10,128 --> 00:01:11,710
out the coincidental deploy.

18
00:01:13,710 --> 00:01:16,025
Now the part that makes this trustworthy.

19
00:01:16,025 --> 00:01:20,655
Every single claim is fact checked by a third model, Nemotron 3 point 5 Lightning,

20
00:01:20,655 --> 00:01:23,477
which sees only that claim and the lines it cites.

21
00:01:23,477 --> 00:01:26,526
These checks run in parallel and take a second or two.

22
00:01:26,526 --> 00:01:30,930
Anything the logs do not prove is flagged instead of quietly ending up in your

23
00:01:30,930 --> 00:01:31,833
incident report.

24
00:01:33,833 --> 00:01:36,795
Click any citation to see the raw log line behind it.

25
00:01:36,795 --> 00:01:41,377
Here the verifier caught the reasoning model quoting a time that is not in the log

26
00:01:41,377 --> 00:01:42,159
line it cited.

27
00:01:42,159 --> 00:01:46,630
Below are prioritized action items, like fixing backup retention and routing the

28
00:01:46,630 --> 00:01:49,983
repository full alert to on call instead of a null receiver.

29
00:01:51,983 --> 00:01:56,651
The usage panel shows how the work was split: many fast, cheap calls to Nano and

30
00:01:56,651 --> 00:01:58,635
Lightning, one deep call to Ultra.

31
00:01:58,635 --> 00:02:02,954
A full run on this incident costs about one or two cents on Token Factory.

32
00:02:02,954 --> 00:02:07,505
The report exports as markdown with an evidence appendix, ready for your wiki.

33
00:02:09,505 --> 00:02:14,583
Postmortem Pilot runs entirely on Nebius Token Factory with three NVIDIA Nemotron

34
00:02:14,583 --> 00:02:19,660
open models, each chosen for the job it does best: Nano to read, Ultra to reason,

35
00:02:19,660 --> 00:02:20,914
Lightning to verify.

36
00:02:20,914 --> 00:02:23,797
The code is open source under the MIT license.

37
00:02:23,797 --> 00:02:25,051
Thanks for watching.
